      <h1>How HTTP evolved</h1>
      <p>A stable request–response idea, carried by changing message formats and network transports.</p>
    </header>
    
      
        <section id="early"><h2>The early Web</h2>
          <p>The earliest HTTP, later called HTTP/0.9, handled a simple GET request and returned a document. It had no status line or header fields. HTTP/1.0 expanded the exchange with version information, metadata, and status codes, making different content types and error handling easier to support. <a href="references.html#rfc1945">[1]</a></p>
          <p>HTTP/1.1 made persistent connections the default, so a connection could serve multiple exchanges. It also developed the rules needed for interoperable messaging and hosting. Its first specification appeared in 1997; RFC 9112 describes the modern HTTP/1.1 message format. <a href="references.html#rfc9112">[2]</a></p>
        </section>
        <section id="versions"><h2>Five stages of HTTP</h2><p>The dates below distinguish early use from specification publication. They do not mark universal adoption.</p>
          <div class="table-container" role="region" aria-label="HTTP version comparison" tabindex="0">
            <table><caption>HTTP versions and their main contributions</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Version</th><th scope="col">Historical marker</th><th scope="col">Main contribution</th><th scope="col">Important limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">HTTP/0.9</th><td>Early 1990s</td><td>A minimal GET request and document response.</td><td>No headers or status codes.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">HTTP/1.0</th><td>RFC 1945, 1996</td><td>Headers, status information, and richer message types.</td><td>Connection reuse was not the default model.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">HTTP/1.1</th><td>First specified in 1997</td><td>Persistent connections and mature message framing.</td><td>Pipelined responses must remain ordered.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">HTTP/2</th><td>First standardized in 2015</td><td>Binary frames, multiplexed streams, and header compression.</td><td>TCP loss can delay all streams on a connection.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">HTTP/3</th><td>RFC 9114, 2022</td><td>HTTP mapped onto QUIC, with independent stream delivery.</td><td>Benefits depend on the network and implementation.</td></tr>
            </tbody></table>
          </div>
          <p class="source-note">Sources: <a href="references.html#rfc1945">[1]</a>, <a href="references.html#rfc9112">[2]</a>, <a href="references.html#rfc9113">[3]</a>, <a href="references.html#rfc9114">[4]</a>.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="performance"><h2>What changed beneath the messages?</h2>
          <h3>HTTP/2: multiple streams on one connection</h3><p>HTTP/2 divides messages into binary frames and interleaves streams. Several requests can progress over one TCP connection. This changes transmission rather than replacing familiar HTTP meanings such as GET and 404. TCP still delivers bytes in order, so a missing packet can hold up otherwise independent streams. <a href="references.html#rfc9113">[3]</a></p>
          <h3>HTTP/3: a different transport</h3><p>HTTP/3 maps HTTP onto QUIC, which runs over UDP and incorporates TLS 1.3. QUIC provides reliable streams. Lost data on one stream need not prevent delivery on another stream, although the connection still shares network capacity. HTTP/3 is not simply “unreliable HTTP” because it uses UDP underneath. <a href="references.html#rfc9114">[4]</a></p>
          <aside class="note"><h3>The lasting idea</h3><p>Versions change framing and transport while preserving shared semantics. A protocol version describes how HTTP travels; HTTPS describes protection of the connection. <a href="references.html#rfc9110">[5]</a></p></aside>
        </section>
        <section id="observe"><h2>Observe an HTTP exchange</h2><ol><li>Open your browser’s developer tools and select the Network panel.</li><li>Reload a page and select a document request.</li><li>Inspect its method, status code, and response headers.</li><li>Enable the Protocol column if available to compare HTTP versions.</li></ol><p>Your result depends on the browser, server, and network. A single observation does not establish which version is fastest.</p></section>
